    {
      "title": "Homophones, homonyms, homographs",
      "html": "<p><b>Homophones</b> \u2014 sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning: <i>their / there / they're; principal / principle; stationery / stationary; bare / bear; peace / piece</i>.</p>\n  <p><b>Homonyms</b> \u2014 same spelling <u>and</u> same sound but different meanings: <i>bank</i> (river / financial); <i>bark</i> (of a tree / of a dog); <i>fair</i> (just / market).</p>\n  <p><b>Homographs</b> \u2014 same spelling but different meaning and often different pronunciation: <i>lead</i> (metal, /led/) vs <i>lead</i> (to guide, /li\u02d0d/); <i>tear</i> (from the eye, /t\u026a\u0259/) vs <i>tear</i> (to rip, /te\u0259/); <i>row</i> (a line) vs <i>row</i> (a quarrel).</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Same sound, different spelling.</li><li>Same spelling, different sound and meaning.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which pair are homophones?<br/><b>A:</b> their / there</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which pair are homographs (differ in sound)?<br/><b>A:</b> lead (metal) / lead (guide)</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Choose the right word: <i>The wound had a (bare / bear) minimum of bandage.</i>",
          "a": "<b>bare</b> = minimum; <i>bear</i> = the animal or 'to endure'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Phrasal verbs, idioms and collocations",
      "html": "<p>A <b>phrasal verb</b> is a verb + particle whose meaning is often not obvious from its parts: <i>put off</i> (postpone), <i>give up</i> (quit), <i>look after</i> (care for), <i>break down</i> (stop functioning; cry).</p>\n  <p>An <b>idiom</b> is a fixed expression whose meaning is not literal: <i>kick the bucket</i> (die), <i>let the cat out of the bag</i> (reveal a secret), <i>burn the midnight oil</i> (work late), <i>a piece of cake</i> (very easy).</p>\n  <p>A <b>collocation</b> is a natural word partnership: <i>make a decision</i> (not 'do a decision'), <i>strong tea</i> (not 'powerful tea'), <i>heavy rain</i>, <i>catch a cold</i>.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>'Fast food' is the fixed collocation.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which pair are correct collocations?<br/><b>A:</b> fast food only</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Give the meaning of <i>burn the midnight oil</i>.",
          "a": "To work or study late into the night."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Registers \u2014 Medicine, Science, ICT",
      "html": "<p><b>Medicine</b>: diagnosis, prognosis, symptom, prescription, dosage, antibiotic, epidemic, surgery, anaesthetic, quarantine, immunisation.</p>\n  <p><b>Science</b> (general): hypothesis, experiment, control, variable, molecule, compound, catalyst, momentum, evaporation, ecosystem.</p>\n  <p><b>ICT</b>: hardware, software, byte, browser, encryption, algorithm, database, malware, cloud, bandwidth, firewall, protocol.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Encryption \u2014 computing/ICT.</li><li>Science.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> <i>Encryption</i> belongs to which register?<br/><b>A:</b> ICT</p><p><b>Q:</b> <i>Hypothesis</i> belongs to which register?<br/><b>A:</b> Science</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li><i>Firewall</i> in ICT is: \u2014 security software / hardware to block threats.</li><li><i>Compound</i> in Science means: \u2014 a substance from two or more elements chemically joined.</li><li><i>Cloud</i> in ICT means: \u2014 internet-hosted computing storage.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which register does <i>algorithm, encryption, malware</i> belong to?",
          "a": "ICT / Computing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which pair are homophones?<br/><b>A:</b> their / there \u2014 Same sound, different spelling.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which pair are homographs (differ in sound)?<br/><b>A:</b> lead (metal) / lead (guide) \u2014 Same spelling, different sound and meaning.</p><p><b>Q:</b> <i>Bank</i> (river bank / money bank) is an example of a:<br/><b>A:</b> homonym \u2014 Same spelling and sound, different meanings.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which pair are homophones?",
          "a": "their / there"
        },
        {
          "q": "Which pair are homographs (differ in sound)?",
          "a": "lead (metal) / lead (guide)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Key definitions",
      "html": "<p>Learn these definitions exactly as stated \u2014 they are the terms examiners use.</p><ul><li><b>Homophones</b>: \u2014 sound the same but differ in spelling and meaning: their / there / they're; principal / principle; stationery / stationary; bare / bear; peace / piece</li><li><b>Homonyms</b>: \u2014 same spelling and same sound but different meanings: bank (river / financial); bark (of a tree / of a dog); fair (just / market)</li><li><b>Homographs</b>: \u2014 same spelling but different meaning and often different pronunciation: lead (metal, /led/) vs lead (to guide, /li\u02d0d/); tear (from the eye, /t\u026a\u0259/) vs tear (to rip, /te\u0259/); row (a</li><li><b>phrasal verb</b>: is a verb + particle whose meaning is often not obvious from its parts: put off (postpone), give up (quit), look after (care for), break down (stop functioning; cry)</li><li><b>idiom</b>: is a fixed expression whose meaning is not literal: kick the bucket (die), let the cat out of the bag (reveal a secret), burn the midnight oil (work late), a piece of cake (very ea</li><li><b>collocation</b>: is a natural word partnership: make a decision (not 'do a decision'), strong tea (not 'powerful tea'), heavy rain , catch a cold</li></ul>",
      "examples": []
    }
  ],
